  "image_name": "1fb2bad884386e5d1d585be74bd10c16.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates two genome-editing strategies used to modify the *pfkelch13* locus, comparing CRISPR–Cas9 with zinc-finger nucleases (ZFN). In both cases, a nuclease cuts the parasite DNA at a chosen site and a donor DNA template supplies the desired sequence change, producing an edited chromosomal locus.\n\nIn the upper part, CRISPR–Cas9 is directed by an sgRNA to the wild-type *pfkelch13* sequence near a PAM site. The donor DNA carries the clinically important PfKelch13-C580Y mutation, along with additional “shield mutations” that appear to alter the target sequence without changing the encoded protein, helping protect the repaired locus from being cut again. The resulting edited DNA contains the new codon corresponding to Y at position 580.\n\nIn the lower part, ZFN editing is shown as an alternative approach targeting the same gene. Here, the donor DNA introduces a different mutation, PfKelch13-R539T, and homologous repair yields a chromosome carrying that edited codon.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes targeted introduction of specific *pfkelch13* mutations by programmable nucleases and donor-directed repair, highlighting how distinct editing platforms can create defined resistance-associated alleles.",
